---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import { publications } from '../data/publications';

const years = [...new Set(publications.map((p) => p.year))].sort((a, b) => b - a);
---

<Base
  title="Research"
  section="blue"
  description="Research on search for teachers, interfaces for domain experts, and retrieval in historical photo collections."
>
  <div class="page wrap">
    <h1 class="page-title">Research</h1>
    <p class="page-intro">
      My work sits between information retrieval and human-computer interaction. The common question is how people with
      specialised knowledge find and analyse material in large collections.
    </p>

    <section>
      <h2 class="section-title">Search for teachers</h2>
      <p>
        My dissertation, "Meeting Teachers where they Search: Novel Web Search Augmentations for Educational Resource
        Discovery", starts from a finding in earlier research: teachers often use general-purpose web search instead of
        specialised repositories. A behavioural study of teachers on educational search tasks showed how they formulate
        queries and which aspects of a resource's relevance they cannot judge from standard search results. A <a href="#llm-relevance-labels">multi-dimensional relevance framework</a> then measured the gap between what search snippets convey and what the
        resources contain. To close that gap at scale, I <a href="#tpdl-2024-metadata-usage">examined how much structured educational metadata exists</a> on the
        web and <a href="#jcdl-2026-metadata">built lightweight models that extract it in real time</a>. The parts come together in <a href="#aied-2025-augmented-search">LessonLens</a>, a browser
        extension that adds educational information to existing web search interfaces, which I evaluated in a user study.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section>
      <h2 class="section-title">Interfaces for domain experts</h2>
      <p>
        <a href="https://projects.tib.eu/grobi">GROBI</a> is a web platform where researchers and librarians build bibliometric analyses by connecting nodes in a
        graph. I designed and implemented it, and I designed, ran and analysed its usability study. <a href="https://www.ifi.uzh.ch/en/vmml/news/Software-VIAN.html">VIAN</a> is a video
        annotation tool for film scholars, developed at the University of Zurich with the Visual Analytics group at TIB.
        The <a href="#diamond-funding-navigator">Diamond Funding Navigator</a> gives libraries and institutions an overview of the consortia that finance
        Diamond Open Access publications. These projects, like the teacher search work, ask what a specialist needs from
        an interface that a general-purpose tool does not give them.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section>
      <h2 class="section-title">Retrieval in historical collections</h2>
      <p>
        Two recent papers examine how CLIP-style models fail on historical photograph collections. One <a href="#clip-failures-historical-photos">uses hierarchical
        controlled vocabularies to locate the failures</a>, and the other <a href="#bias-vlm-colonial-contexts">measures gender and racial bias in colonial
        contexts</a>. Earlier, I worked with a historian and linguists on about 55,000 pages of 1970s refugee-policy documents
        from UK and US government archives and the UNHCR. My part was the object detection that finds stamps and extracts
        metadata from the scans. The <a href="#dsh-2021-refugee-archives">resulting paper</a> uses topic modelling to follow how each organisation's focus
        changed over time.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section id="publications">
      <h2 class="section-title">Publications</h2>
      {
        years.map((year) => (
          <div class="year">
            <h3>{year}</h3>
            <ul>
              {publications
                .filter((p) => p.year === year)
                .map((p) => (
                  <li id={p.slug}>
                    <span class="title">{p.url ? <a href={p.url}>{p.title}</a> : p.title}</span>
                    <span class="authors">
                      {p.authors.map((a, i) => (
                        <>
                          {a === 'Ratan Sebastian' ? <strong>{a}</strong> : a}
                          {i < p.authors.length - 1 ? ', ' : ''}
                        </>
                      ))}
                    </span>
                    {p.venue && <span class="venue">{p.venue}</span>}
                    {p.award && <span class="award">{p.award}</span>}
                    {p.code && (
                      <span class="code">
                        <a href={p.code.url}>{p.code.label}</a>
                      </span>
                    )}
                  </li>
                ))}
            </ul>
          </div>
        ))
      }
    </section>
  </div>
</Base>

<style>
  .year {
    display: grid;
    gap: 0.75rem 2rem;
    padding-block: 1.25rem;
    border-top: 0.25rem solid var(--black);
  }

  @media (min-width: 700px) {
    .year {
      grid-template-columns: 6rem 1fr;
    }
  }

  h3 {
    font-size: 2rem;
  }

  ul {
    list-style: none;
    margin: 0;
    padding: 0;
    display: grid;
    gap: 1.25rem;
  }

  li {
    display: grid;
    gap: 0.15rem;
    max-width: 44rem;
    line-height: 1.4;
  }

  li {
    scroll-margin-top: 1.5rem;
  }

  li:target {
    background: var(--yellow);
    outline: 0.5rem solid var(--yellow);
  }

  .title {
    font-weight: 600;
  }

  .venue {
    font-style: italic;
  }

  .code {
    font-family: var(--display);
    font-weight: 600;
    font-size: 0.95rem;
  }

  .award {
    justify-self: start;
    margin-top: 0.3rem;
    padding: 0.1rem 0.5rem;
    background: var(--yellow);
    font-family: var(--display);
    font-weight: 700;
    font-size: 0.95rem;
  }
</style>
